ev('EVD059',"Hynek (1972, p.233) prints the log as calls 'received at the Blue Book offices by Lieutenant Anspaugh' (Dayton, Ohio), and the book's version matches it. The call times are therefore the Blue Book (Eastern) clock. The batch of events passed on in the 4:40 call (H-2 3:00; B-4 3:17; 3:20-3:40) all precede that call, which is only consistent if they are on the same clock: if they were Warren local (MDT, 1-2 h behind Eastern) they would postdate the 4:40 call. Inference: the whole log is most probably Eastern (EDT or EST) — it is not Mountain time. This resolves the time-zone question except for EST vs EDT.",'DOCUMENT_ANALYSIS','SRC037 pp.233-235','INC033;EVD056','8834','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD060',"Hynek's commentary: Blue Book dismissed the Midwest flap as 'stars seen through inversion layers'; Quintanilla said the Warren sightings were 'nothing but stars!' (CLM100 now matched to Hynek's text); Hynek argues inversions cannot make stars move through a considerable arc. But he then sets out two competing hypotheses — a cover-up, and a 'foul-up' based on the premise that the reports could not have a basis in fact — and adds that Blue Book's 'lack of interest belies any charge of cover-up; they just didn't care.' The book (Ch.10, Ch.16) uses the 'stars' quote and calls the Warren handling 'patently absurd,' but not Hynek's lack-of-interest reading.",'DOCUMENT_QUOTATION','SRC037','CLM100;CLM101;CLM095','8905','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD061',"CORRECTION to EVD055 and updated sky test (analysis/warren_sky_by_entry.py; stars/planets incl. the Pleiades; times shifted by hypothesis H0 Mountain, H1 EST, H2 EDT). Eastern readings (H1/H2, the likely ones per EVD059): '3:00 white oval directly overhead' -> Deneb at 78-86 deg altitude (H0: no bright object >70 deg); '3:20 seven UFOs east' and '3:25 six stacked vertically' -> the Pleiades (six-seven naked-eye stars) low in the ENE at 8-19 deg (and Aldebaran 4-5 deg under H1); '3:40 one at 70 deg azimuth' -> Pleiades/Aldebaran at az 68-77 deg; '4:00 four NW, three NE' -> Vega/Deneb (WNW) and Capella plus the Pleiades (NE). Jupiter only matters under Mountain time (H0), which EVD059 makes unlikely. My earlier statement that the match 'fails' under Eastern time was wrong because I omitted the Pleiades and Aldebaran.",'ANALYST_CALCULATION','analysis/warren_sky_by_entry.py','INC033;EXP032;EVD055','8834','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD062',"Log entries NOT explained by stars/planets in my test: (a) 3:17 'oval and white with white lines on its sides and a flashing red light in its center moving east ... descending ... landed 10 miles east' — describes an aircraft with anti-collision beacon and wing lights more than a star; (b) 3:40 'one at 120 deg' — no bright star/planet near azimuth 120 deg at 1:40-3:40 MDT under any hypothesis (Saturn is at ~140 deg); (c) '4:00 four NW' — only Vega and Deneb are bright NW; (d) 'three came from the east, stacked vertically, passed through the other two, all five heading west' — an apparent-motion description that would require illusory motion (autokinesis/scintillation) or aircraft; (e) 1:30 'large circular object emitting several colors ... moved rapidly to the northeast' (Cheyenne civilians, 2330-0130 MDT) — Capella low in the NE is the only bright star there; aircraft/meteors/satellites more likely.",'ANALYST_ASSESSMENT','SRC037 log; warren_sky_by_entry.py','INC033;EXP032','8834','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD063',"Satellite (Echo 2) test NOT done: no 1965 orbital elements were available to me. Feasibility note: Echo 2 (~1,000-1,300 km altitude) can be sunlit overhead only while the sun is < ~33 deg below the horizon; at Cheyenne on 1 Aug the sun reaches ~-31 deg at ~0100 MDT, so passes between ~2300 and ~0200 MDT would be marginal and after ~0200 MDT well lit. Whether any pass coincided with a log entry requires historical TLEs (Space-Track login).",'METHODOLOGICAL_LIMIT','analyst reasoning','INC033;EXP032','8834','MEDIUM','INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE')
